# First-party SHACL Rules case: LAYERED stratified execution (SPARQL 1.2 RL §6.5
# "Evaluation of a Rule Set", over the SHACL Advanced Features rule vocabulary).
#
# Two rules on the same shape, in two different strata:
#
#   sh:order 0 — tag every ex:Person with `ex:tag ex:seen` (ungated).
#   sh:order 1 — assert `ex:untagged true`, but ONLY for a focus node conforming
#                to ex:Untagged, which demands ZERO ex:tag values (sh:maxCount 0).
#
# Stratification runs the order-0 layer to completion and MATERIALIZES its
# inferences before the order-1 layer reads the graph. By the time the order-1
# rule evaluates its sh:condition, ex:alice already carries `ex:tag ex:seen`, so
# the condition FAILS and the order-1 head is never derived.
#
# This is the case that proves sh:order is not inert: a single global fixpoint
# over one flat rule list evaluates both rules against the SAME unmaterialized
# round graph, sees ex:alice untagged, and wrongly derives `ex:untagged true`.
# Swapping the two sh:order values yields a DIFFERENT inferred set — pinned by the
# companion Rust test `swapping_orders_changes_the_inferred_set`.
#
# Expected derived set: exactly one triple (ex:alice ex:tag ex:seen).

@prefix ex:  <http://example.org/ns#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix sh:  <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .

ex:alice rdf:type ex:Person .

ex:Untagged
  rdf:type sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:property [ sh:path ex:tag ; sh:maxCount 0 ] .

ex:S
  rdf:type sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass ex:Person ;
  sh:rule [
      rdf:type sh:TripleRule ;
      sh:order 0 ;
      sh:subject sh:this ;
      sh:predicate ex:tag ;
      sh:object ex:seen ] ;
  sh:rule [
      rdf:type sh:TripleRule ;
      sh:order 1 ;
      sh:condition ex:Untagged ;
      sh:subject sh:this ;
      sh:predicate ex:untagged ;
      sh:object true ] .
